.TH EXPAND 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
expand \- convert tabs to spaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expand
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR N ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B expand
copies files to standard output, replacing each tab with the spaces that
reach the next tab stop.
Tab stops are every 8 columns, or every
.I N
columns with
.BR \-t .
It reads each
.I file
in order, and reads standard input when there are no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- .
.PP
Bytes other than tabs and NULs are copied unchanged, including carriage
returns.
Line endings are not converted, and a last line without a newline gets
none.
.BR unexpand (1)
converts in the other direction.
.PP
A tab pads the text before it on the line to the next multiple of
.I N
columns, and a tab that is already at a tab stop moves to the following
one.
After a tab, the count continues from that tab stop.
Text is measured in columns, not bytes.
In UTF-8 text, a wide character such as an ideograph is 2 columns, a
combining or other zero-width character is 0, and an invalid sequence
is 1.
In single-byte text, every byte is 1 column; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
Every control character is 1 column, including backspace, carriage
return, and escape.
Backspace does not move the column back, and the bytes of an escape
sequence count.
.PP
A NUL byte is deleted from the output and starts the count over, as if
a new line began there.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-it4 ,
and the argument of
.B \-t
may be attached, as in
.BR \-t4 ,
or be the next argument.
Long options may be abbreviated to a unique prefix, and the argument of
.B \-\-tabs
may follow
.B =
or be the next argument.
Options may follow operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
If
.B \-t
is repeated, the last one applies.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-initial
Convert only tabs at the start of a line.
Conversion stops at the first character that is not a space or tab, and
later tabs are copied unchanged.
Spaces and tabs before it count toward the column.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-tabs=\fIN\fR
Set tab stops every
.I N
columns.
The default is 8.
.I N
is a decimal integer from 1 to 4294967295, with no sign, prefix, or
suffix, and a leading zero does not make it octal.
It is a single number, not a list.
Any other value is an error, reported before any input is read.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded when
.B expand
starts, as needed when it is run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first of these that is set, even to an empty value, decides how text
is measured.
If its value is
.BR C ,
text is single bytes, each 1 column, and output to a console is not
converted.
Otherwise text is UTF-8 when the ANSI code page is UTF-8, and single
bytes when it is not.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown option, a missing option argument, or an invalid
.IR N ;
a
.I file
that could not be opened or read, including a directory; or a failure
writing standard output.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited.
.PP
Error messages begin with
.B expand:
and go to standard error.
An unknown option or a missing option argument also prints the usage
message.
The remaining files are still processed after one cannot be opened or
read.
.SH EXAMPLES
Expand the tabs in a stream:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqa\etb\en\etc\en\(aq | expand
a       b
        c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Put tab stops every 4 columns, writing a new file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ expand \-t 4 main.c >main4.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Replace indentation tabs in a file, leaving the tabs inside lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ expand \-i main.c >main.tmp && mv main.tmp main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Expand several files into one:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ expand a.txt b.txt >both.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU expand:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-t
takes one number, not a list of tab stops.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BI \- N
form of
.BR \-t ,
and there is no
.BR \-\-version .
.IP \(bu 2
Backspace is one column.
GNU expand moves the column back.
.IP \(bu 2
NUL bytes are deleted.
GNU expand keeps them.
.PP
A tab interval above 2147483647 is accepted but does not pad correctly,
because the padding width is a signed int.
.PP
Files and standard streams are binary, with no CRLF translation.
The ANSI code page of busybox is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, so text is UTF-8 there unless one of the variables above says
otherwise.
.PP
Each
.I file
may be a Windows path, with a drive letter and either kind of slash, or
a busybox-w32 device name such as
.IR /dev/null .
On Windows 10 version 1903 or later, file names may contain any Unicode
characters, and on older systems only those of the system code page.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR unexpand (1)
